<h2><strong>Origins of International Students Day</strong></h2>
<p>The history of this day is rooted in resistance and remembrance. On November 17, 1939, Nazi troops raided the University of Prague following peaceful protests led by Czech students. These demonstrations opposed the German occupation of Czechoslovakia and mourned the death of Jan Opletal, a student protester.</p>
<p>In response, Nazi forces executed nine student leaders, arrested over 1,200 students, and sent them to concentration camps. Soon after, all Czech universities and colleges were forcibly closed.</p>
<p>One year later, exiled members of the Central Association of Czechoslovak Students reformed their group in London, working with the British National Union of Students. They designated November 17 as a date of international observance in memory of the tragedy.</p>
<h2><strong>Why International Students Day Still Matters</strong></h2>
<p>International Students Day in 2025 stands as both a solemn remembrance and a spirited acknowledgment of the power of student voices. While rooted in protest, the day has grown into a celebration of community, education, and global dialogue.</p>
